caption: Looking up a related tiddler at import
tags: $:/plugins/crosseye/json-convert-studio/usage/recipes
title: $:/plugins/crosseye/json-convert-studio/usage/recipes/lookup-at-import
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

A wikitext transform runs with the whole wiki in scope, so a binding
can set a field by looking something up rather than copying it.  The
motivating case: index cards carry a student's initials, and a roster
tiddler per student carries those initials in an `init` field under an
anonymous title.  The import should record the roster title, not the
initials.

```
title: Student ID
name: student-id
tags: $:/tags/json-convert/transform
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

<$text text={{{ [<value>listed[init]removeprefix[—]] }}}/>
```

```
"custom-fields": {
  "initials": "{{initials_or_name}}",
  "student":  "{{initials_or_name|student-id}}"
}
```

Initials the roster does not know come out as an empty `student`
field; the readable initials stay in their own field for checking
afterwards.

''Keying on more than one field.''  Every transform also receives the
whole record — as the `record` variable in wikitext, holding the
record as JSON text — so a lookup can qualify by a second field.  Bind
`{{@record|student-id}}` and read what you need inside:

```
<$text text={{{ [<record>jsonget[initials]] :filter[<record>jsonget[class]match[101]] +[listed[init]removeprefix[—]] }}}/>
```

The same shape serves a citation string looked up against a
bibliography, or any locally important relational key.  Two cautions:
the lookup target must exist in the wiki the import runs in, which for
an importer pack means the recipient's wiki; and a lookup that finds
nothing yields '', so pair it with `default[…]` if a placeholder is
better than a blank.
